async function promisePool(tasks, limit) {
  const results = [];
  const queue = [...tasks];

  const worker = async () => {
    while (queue.length > 0) {
      const task = queue.shift();
      results.push(await task());
    }
  };

  await Promise.all(Array.from({ length: limit }, worker));
  return results;
}

referenceAnswer.en:
The workers dequeue in input order but `push` in completion order. With `limit = 2` and a first task that takes 500 ms while the second takes 10 ms, `results[0]` is the second task's value. The position has to be captured when the task is taken, not when it settles:

```javascript
async function promisePool(tasks, limit) {
  const results = new Array(tasks.length);
  let next = 0;

  const worker = async () => {
    while (next < tasks.length) {
      const i = next++;
      results[i] = await tasks[i]();
    }
  };

  await Promise.all(Array.from({ length: Math.min(limit, tasks.length) }, worker));
  return results;
}
```

`next++` is safe without a lock because the read-increment pair runs synchronously between two `await` points, and JavaScript is single-threaded. Clamping the worker count with `Math.min` avoids spawning idle workers when `tasks.length < limit`. Note also that a single rejecting task rejects the whole pool while the other workers keep draining the queue — wrap `tasks[i]()` in a try/catch and store an outcome object if you want `allSettled` semantics.

